1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,500
Agra.

2
00:00:01,970 --> 00:00:04,870
August, sixteen sixty-six.

3
00:00:05,570 --> 00:00:09,770
Around one house in the Mughal city,
a ring of guards stands watch.

4
00:00:10,430 --> 00:00:14,170
One letter from Agra puts
that watch at a thousand men.

5
00:00:14,870 --> 00:00:17,330
Inside are a Maratha
ruler from the Deccan...

6
00:00:17,950 --> 00:00:19,710
and his nine-year-old son.

7
00:00:20,410 --> 00:00:23,920
Then, one morning, word
races through the city.

8
00:00:24,710 --> 00:00:26,380
They are gone.

9
00:00:26,990 --> 00:00:32,440
How do you vanish from under the eyes
of one of the richest empires on earth?

10
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:34,680
You may know the famous answer.

11
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:37,100
Baskets of sweets.

12
00:00:37,770 --> 00:00:41,770
But letters written in Agra
that very summer still survive.

13
00:00:42,470 --> 00:00:43,940
So let's check the legend...

14
00:00:44,690 --> 00:00:46,150
against the record.

15
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:48,310
First, the players.

16
00:00:48,930 --> 00:00:53,680
Shivaji: the Maratha leader who carved a
kingdom out of the hill forts of western

17
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:57,300
India, and spent years
raiding Mughal territory.

18
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,600
Facing him, the emperor, Aurangzeb.

19
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:03,860
And the man who would
bring them face to face...

20
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:08,290
Aurangzeb's veteran general,
Mirza Raja Jai Singh.

21
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:14,650
In sixteen sixty-five, Jai Singh's
army closed in on Shivaji's forts.

22
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:19,360
Rather than lose everything,
Shivaji made a deal at Purandar.

23
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:22,560
Twenty-three forts went to the empire.

24
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:25,530
He kept twelve, including his stronghold,

25
00:01:25,780 --> 00:01:26,680
Rajgad.

26
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:29,040
But Jai Singh wanted more.

27
00:01:29,740 --> 00:01:34,240
Shivaji should travel north, and
stand before the emperor in person.

28
00:01:34,930 --> 00:01:37,990
Jai Singh swore that no
harm would come to him.

29
00:01:38,490 --> 00:01:41,960
And at court, his son,
the prince Ram Singh,

30
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:43,540
gave his word as well.

31
00:01:44,490 --> 00:01:46,040
Remember that promise.

32
00:01:46,650 --> 00:01:52,450
In March sixteen sixty-six, Shivaji
set out from Rajgad with his son,

33
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:56,040
Sambhaji, and a small but splendid escort.

34
00:01:56,740 --> 00:01:58,710
In May, they reached Agra.

35
00:01:59,410 --> 00:02:02,880
One of Ram Singh's officers
described him in a letter home.

36
00:02:03,830 --> 00:02:04,730
Lean.

37
00:02:05,070 --> 00:02:05,970
Short.

38
00:02:06,470 --> 00:02:07,370
Fair.

39
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:08,680
Bearded.

40
00:02:09,010 --> 00:02:12,680
And, he wrote, "even without
finding out who he is,

41
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:17,080
one does feel instinctively
that he is a ruler of men."

42
00:02:17,690 --> 00:02:18,820
The twelfth of May.

43
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:22,810
The emperor's fiftieth birthday,
by the lunar calendar.

44
00:02:23,510 --> 00:02:26,340
Ram Singh led Shivaji into the fort.

45
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:29,440
Shivaji presented his gifts of gold...

46
00:02:30,190 --> 00:02:31,090
and waited.

47
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:32,860
Nothing.

48
00:02:33,270 --> 00:02:36,450
The emperor did not say
a single word to him.

49
00:02:37,150 --> 00:02:41,310
Shivaji was led back, to stand
behind rows of other nobles.

50
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:45,380
Then robes of honour went
to princes, to ministers...

51
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:51,550
and to Maharaja Jaswant Singh, a general
who had once besieged one of Shivaji's

52
00:02:51,590 --> 00:02:52,490
forts...

53
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:53,900
and failed.

54
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:56,560
For Shivaji, it was too much.

55
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:59,490
According to a letter
from Ram Singh's staff,

56
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:02,620
he told the prince, "I
cast off your mansab."

57
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:04,660
His imperial rank.

58
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:06,990
Then he turned his back on the throne...

59
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:09,150
and walked away.

60
00:03:09,810 --> 00:03:12,530
At a court where nobles
were expected to wait,

61
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:16,410
as one English trader put it,
"with their hands before them,

62
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:20,450
like mutes," it was a
stunning act of defiance.

63
00:03:21,150 --> 00:03:22,900
Officials brought him a robe of honour.

64
00:03:23,650 --> 00:03:24,610
He refused it.

65
00:03:25,220 --> 00:03:26,770
Now the court turned on him.

66
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:30,260
Powerful voices, the emperor's own sister,

67
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:33,890
Jahanara, among them, demanded punishment.

68
00:03:34,590 --> 00:03:38,260
A letter from Agra reports that
the emperor weighed his options.

69
00:03:39,010 --> 00:03:40,050
Kill Shivaji...

70
00:03:40,830 --> 00:03:42,550
or lock him away in a fortress.

71
00:03:43,250 --> 00:03:44,840
Then Ram Singh stepped in.

72
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:47,670
His father had guaranteed
Shivaji's safety,

73
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:48,800
he argued.

74
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:54,010
If the emperor wanted Shivaji dead, he
would have to kill Ram Singh first.

75
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:56,390
The emperor's answer was simple.

76
00:03:57,050 --> 00:03:58,730
Then sign a security bond.

77
00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:00,920
If Shivaji escapes...

78
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:03,110
you will answer for it.

79
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:04,920
Ram Singh signed.

80
00:04:05,870 --> 00:04:07,750
Shivaji was not executed...

81
00:04:08,450 --> 00:04:10,090
but he was not free.

82
00:04:10,810 --> 00:04:13,650
At one point, he was
ordered to march to Kabul,

83
00:04:13,930 --> 00:04:16,490
on the far north-western frontier.

84
00:04:17,190 --> 00:04:21,160
Days later, after petitions,
and payments to courtiers,

85
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:22,710
the order was dropped.

86
00:04:23,410 --> 00:04:26,610
Troops of the city's
police chief, Fulad Khan,

87
00:04:26,970 --> 00:04:28,220
ringed his quarters.

88
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:32,450
Ram Singh's own Rajputs
watched his bed at night.

89
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:34,790
The emperor's guest...

90
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:37,270
had become his prisoner.

91
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:39,960
So Shivaji tried everything.

92
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:45,120
Two crore rupees, twenty million,
if his forts were returned.

93
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:48,840
An offer to fight for the
emperor against Bijapur.

94
00:04:49,540 --> 00:04:53,240
He even asked to go to Benares,
and become a holy man.

95
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:55,460
The reply came back.

96
00:04:56,160 --> 00:04:58,260
Let him become a fakir, then...

97
00:04:58,870 --> 00:05:02,070
and live in Allahabad fort, under guard.

98
00:05:02,750 --> 00:05:06,850
And then came a blow from the very
man who had promised his safety.

99
00:05:07,550 --> 00:05:11,180
In July, Jai Singh wrote to
his son from the Deccan.

100
00:05:11,880 --> 00:05:16,580
It was, he said, "not at all
politic" to let Shivaji come home.

101
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:18,380
Keep him in Agra.

102
00:05:19,130 --> 00:05:20,030
Treat him well...

103
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:21,620
but keep him.

104
00:05:22,140 --> 00:05:24,190
Shivaji's money was running out.

105
00:05:24,890 --> 00:05:28,480
He borrowed sixty-six thousand
rupees from Ram Singh.

106
00:05:28,990 --> 00:05:32,220
And over the summer, he
sent his own escort home,

107
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:34,750
leaving himself almost alone.

108
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:38,240
To the court, it looked like surrender.

109
00:05:38,920 --> 00:05:40,210
It wasn't surrender.

110
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:42,190
It was preparation.

111
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:44,370
Shivaji fell ill...

112
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:46,290
or seemed to.

113
00:05:46,990 --> 00:05:50,850
Soon, large baskets of sweets
began to leave his quarters.

114
00:05:51,430 --> 00:05:55,100
Gifts for Brahmins, holy
men, and courtiers.

115
00:05:55,810 --> 00:05:59,550
Each one hung from a pole,
carried by two porters.

116
00:06:00,250 --> 00:06:03,530
At first, the guards opened
them, and found sweets.

117
00:06:04,450 --> 00:06:05,590
After a few days...

118
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:08,000
they stopped opening them.

119
00:06:08,610 --> 00:06:11,630
Then, one evening in the
third week of August,

120
00:06:11,910 --> 00:06:13,890
the plan goes into motion.

121
00:06:14,590 --> 00:06:17,860
According to later accounts,
Shivaji's half-brother,

122
00:06:18,220 --> 00:06:21,910
Hiroji Farzand, lies
down on Shivaji's bed,

123
00:06:22,170 --> 00:06:23,140
under a quilt.

124
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:27,970
One arm is left showing, with
Shivaji's gold bracelet on the wrist.

125
00:06:28,670 --> 00:06:31,710
A boy sits by the bed, massaging his feet.

126
00:06:32,410 --> 00:06:36,380
And inside the baskets, among
others filled with real sweets,

127
00:06:36,710 --> 00:06:38,080
crouch Shivaji...

128
00:06:38,770 --> 00:06:39,670
and his son.

129
00:06:40,260 --> 00:06:42,480
The porters lift the poles.

130
00:06:43,020 --> 00:06:44,610
They walk past the guards.

131
00:06:45,350 --> 00:06:46,250
And out...

132
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:47,770
into the dark.

133
00:06:48,380 --> 00:06:49,860
How long did the trick hold?

134
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:54,180
The chronicles say the guards were
fooled for most of the next day,

135
00:06:54,530 --> 00:06:58,830
until the house fell strangely
quiet, and they found the bed empty.

136
00:06:59,530 --> 00:07:02,890
The letters written at the
time suggest it was quicker.

137
00:07:03,590 --> 00:07:07,090
One, written in Agra and dated
the eighteenth of August,

138
00:07:07,410 --> 00:07:11,710
says simply: "This morning
Shivaji was found to have fled."

139
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:13,940
Agra erupted.

140
00:07:14,390 --> 00:07:19,440
Ram Singh's own staff wrote home, urging
that every pass and river crossing be

141
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:25,210
watched, and that anyone dressed as a yogi
or a sannyasi be questioned and searched.

142
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:28,880
Fulad Khan, whose men
had ringed the house,

143
00:07:29,190 --> 00:07:33,270
told the emperor the escape had happened
on Ram Singh's side of the watch.

144
00:07:33,770 --> 00:07:38,650
Ram Singh's men were seized and threatened
until they said their master had helped.

145
00:07:39,350 --> 00:07:41,120
Some later took it back.

146
00:07:41,770 --> 00:07:47,280
The emperor, furious, cut Ram Singh's
rank and took away his lands.

147
00:07:47,890 --> 00:07:48,790
So...

148
00:07:49,010 --> 00:07:50,540
was it really the baskets?

149
00:07:51,250 --> 00:07:56,410
About two weeks after the escape, one of
Ram Singh's officers wrote that the trick

150
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:57,440
had become known.

151
00:07:58,140 --> 00:08:02,190
Shivaji had slipped out "by
crouching down in the baskets."

152
00:08:02,890 --> 00:08:06,830
Later Mughal and Maratha
histories tell the same story.

153
00:08:07,470 --> 00:08:10,260
But the Mughal court
suspected inside help,

154
00:08:10,530 --> 00:08:11,430
too.

155
00:08:11,670 --> 00:08:15,190
And a European writer of
the time, Niccolao Manucci,

156
00:08:15,580 --> 00:08:19,490
claimed that Ram Singh himself
had quietly arranged it.

157
00:08:20,220 --> 00:08:23,060
With frightened guards
and partisan witnesses,

158
00:08:23,410 --> 00:08:26,420
we may never know exactly
who looked the other way.

159
00:08:27,030 --> 00:08:28,500
What happened next...

160
00:08:29,250 --> 00:08:30,980
grew into legend.

161
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:36,030
For generations, Shivaji's flight
was told as a long pilgrimage,

162
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:39,590
through Benares, Gaya, even Puri.

163
00:08:40,290 --> 00:08:43,220
The chronicles say he
smeared himself with ash,

164
00:08:43,570 --> 00:08:45,900
disguised as a wandering ascetic.

165
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:51,530
But in nineteen thirty-nine, letters
were found in the Jaipur archives.

166
00:08:52,150 --> 00:08:57,850
Spy reports quoted in them said Shivaji
reached his fort just twenty-five days

167
00:08:57,890 --> 00:08:59,690
after slipping out of Agra.

168
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:05,880
The historian Jadunath Sarkar, who had
once told the long version himself,

169
00:09:06,230 --> 00:09:09,560
now called those pilgrimage
stories fiction.

170
00:09:10,260 --> 00:09:14,670
Twenty-five days, over more
than a thousand kilometres,

171
00:09:14,980 --> 00:09:18,570
meant hard, fast travel on hidden paths.

172
00:09:19,420 --> 00:09:20,390
And it cost him.

173
00:09:21,090 --> 00:09:24,060
He lay ill for weeks after he got home.

174
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:25,660
And the boy?

175
00:09:26,310 --> 00:09:27,800
What became of Sambhaji?

176
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:31,630
Maratha accounts say he was
left behind in Mathura,

177
00:09:31,910 --> 00:09:33,670
with a trusted Brahmin family.

178
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:39,490
The spy reports that reached the emperor
even said that Shivaji's son had died on

179
00:09:39,530 --> 00:09:40,430
the road.

180
00:09:41,110 --> 00:09:42,010
That rumour...

181
00:09:42,680 --> 00:09:44,000
may have protected him.

182
00:09:44,710 --> 00:09:47,970
Months later, Sambhaji
travelled home in disguise,

183
00:09:48,340 --> 00:09:51,610
passed off as a young relative
of the family sheltering him.

184
00:09:52,220 --> 00:09:55,380
The escape changed the
course of the Deccan wars.

185
00:09:56,080 --> 00:10:01,500
Within four years, Shivaji was taking back
the forts he had surrendered at Purandar.

186
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:07,360
In sixteen seventy-four, he was
crowned Chhatrapati at Raigad.

187
00:10:08,070 --> 00:10:11,280
And in a collection of sayings
attributed to Aurangzeb,

188
00:10:11,570 --> 00:10:14,840
the emperor laments that
one moment of carelessness,

189
00:10:15,170 --> 00:10:20,510
the flight of Shivaji, dragged him into
campaigns that lasted to the end of his

190
00:10:20,550 --> 00:10:21,450
days.

191
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:22,860
One night.

192
00:10:23,380 --> 00:10:25,200
A few baskets of sweets.

193
00:10:25,930 --> 00:10:27,020
And Ram Singh?

194
00:10:27,700 --> 00:10:32,220
The prince who paid for the escape
with his rank was later sent east,

195
00:10:32,510 --> 00:10:34,890
to lead a Mughal army into Assam.

196
00:10:35,590 --> 00:10:40,860
There, in sixteen seventy-one,
on the Brahmaputra at Saraighat,

197
00:10:41,170 --> 00:10:45,620
he would face an Ahom general
named Lachit Borphukan.

198
00:10:46,570 --> 00:10:47,470
But that...

199
00:10:47,830 --> 00:10:50,440
is a story for another episode.
